# Our Path of Struggle
Tamil title: நமது போர்ப்பாதை

## Part 1

“One who fears to speak the truth so that the grief of suffering ordinary people may end and they may find happiness is no hero. Those who abandon their vow to defend truth and speak falsehood because stating what happened may bring swelling blame and a flood of hardships—we shall always proclaim them slaves upon this earth.”

In accordance with these words, we describe the realities of our social condition. Speaking for people who lie fallen and degraded, we need not be surprised when the powerful and privileged condemn us.

We trust that truth will triumph. Faith is a good force and a staff supporting our efforts. Yet we do not stop with the phrase “truth will triumph,” for we have often seen falsehood win. We therefore set aside anxiety about victory and regard concern for duty as greater. Disregarding mountain-like opposition, pit-like conspiracies, whirlpool-like fraud and deception blowing like a gale, we labour to uproot Aryan religion and the false tales called puranas and epics.

### We Will Not Halt Our Journey

A soldier crossing a trench and iron barbed wire on the battlefield may see a body buried in earth, gnawed by worms, stripped of clothing and flesh, and lying hideously disfigured. He knows it is the fate of one who went before him upon the same mission. Yet he does not halt. His journey may carry him into the realm of death, or he may reach the city of victory. But if he stops and retreats, where will he arrive? At the settlement of cowards. Will a warrior dare go there?

Likewise, revolutions have been suppressed, armies shattered and the Jains' victory made impermanent. Aryanism, which hid like a snake in its hole while the Buddha lived, attacked and destroyed his followers after him. We know many reformers became weary when their speech bore no fruit. Nevertheless, we refuse to stop our journey.

We continue saying that the Hindu religion created and interpreted by Aryans is unacceptable. Savarkars may travel about to suppress us and Moonjes may scowl, but we shall not sit in a corner. Why? The distress of suffering ordinary people must be relieved and truth must be spoken. Abandoning truth for falsehood through fear of blame is cowardice. Therefore we continue the battle: unmoved by our enemies' atrocities and untroubled even when some of our own people become confused and collide with us.

The Aryans of this country tried to use nationalism and failed. They now put on the great armour called Hinduism, bring warriors such as Savarkar and Moonje before us, and ask, “What do you say now?”

We examine who has arrived, what his method and weapon are, and for whom he fights. Then we begin battle.

### Why Did Shivaji's Rule Collapse?

Savarkar came to Madras and spoke with honeyed enthusiasm about the splendour of Shivaji's Maratha empire. Using that as his pretext, he attacked Muslims.

We understood that he wished to attack Muslims, took a chapter of history as his weapon and fought by enchanting people with Maratha heroism. We therefore showed, from the very history narrated by Aryans, the last page of his chapter: who caused Shivaji's empire to collapse?

He evaded the question. His numerous disciples have still not answered. It is not too late, so we ask again: was Aryanism not responsible for the destruction of Shivaji's empire? Should such Aryanism be cultivated? Is there an answer? We see none.

### Dr Moonje's Message

The elderly sacred-thread-wearing Brahmin Dr Moonje has entered Madras and issued a message.

At the Hindu Theological School—which, like the tale *Apoorva Chintamani Who Took a Thousand Heads*, performed the “noble task” of sending more than a thousand Tamils to prison without thought or hesitation—Moonje reportedly spoke on 7 August about Hinduism's greatness.

Moonje was once in Congress and went to prison. He has now left Congress and belongs to the Hindu Mahasabha. Realising that Congress would not fulfil his demand or ideal, he departed and now condemns Congress and fumes at Gandhi's conduct.

Just as Moonje left Congress, Mr Jinnah left it and became leader of the Muslim League. Likewise Periyar Ramasamy left Congress and became a Dravidian leader. No one will think they departed through fear of hardship or loss; their lives testify otherwise.

### The Desires of Those Who Departed

Moonje's desire is that Aryans prosper.

Jinnah's ideal is that Muslims live well.

Periyar Ramasamy's ideal is that Dravidians live well.

What three methods do they offer?

Moonje says the country must return to the ancient Vedic age.

Jinnah says Muslims need territory and government of their own.

Periyar says Dravidians must awaken, recover their place and homeland, and live.

Moonje summons as witnesses the Rig, Sama, Yajur and Atharva Vedas; lawgivers such as Manu and Yajnavalkya; poets such as Kalidasa; the incarnations Rama and Krishna; and the rishis. Speaking of them, he exhorts: “You are their descendants, people once in that exalted condition. Labour to escape today's state and recover your former position. Arise!”

Jinnah points to the Prophet's principles and world history and asks Muslims to become warriors of progress.

Periyar cites the foundational principle “One community and one God,” the teachings of the Kural, rational inquiry, world conditions and ancient Tamil life. He thunders: “Dravidians, you who once ruled became slaves. Now regain the ability and land to govern. Fear not!”

All three address their respective societies directly, state their ideas plainly and fight.

Gandhi enters between them, pulls a few people from here and a few from there, and says he will arrange profit for everyone. He retreats to a corner when Muslims protest, struggles against Dravidian opposition, and says “yes, master” when Aryans resist. Wearing the costume of a yogi in politics, he remains in an ashram, speaks what should not be spoken and lives a pitiable life.

The army hunting office asks only whose ceremonial fan it must wave at each moment to mesmerise the public. It believes victory will come by shouting “Jai!”

The people open their eyes and mouths wide at these changing spectacles, rush towards whichever side makes the greatest noise, and are tossed about.

What must intellectuals do? Think today; tomorrow we shall write about it.

(*Viduthalai*, 9 August 1940)

## Part 2

Fear must disappear. People must walk with heads held high. There must be no division. They must live with shoulders upright, drawing from the spring of truth and possessing clarity of mind. Elevated thought and vigorous action must arise. That alone is a free country. May my country attain that state.

Rabindranath Tagore expresses this thought in *Gitanjali*, and Swami Vipulananda has pictured it in classical Tamil:

“Where the mind stands without fear;

where all hold their heads high;

where the earth is not broken into fragments;

where words emerge from the spring of truth;

where tireless effort stretches forth its good arms;

where the clear stream of reason crosses the red desert of barren custom and flows long through the waters of knowledge;

where, by Your command, the mind is led into ever-widening thought and advancing action—

Father, command my country to arise in that immortal land of true freedom.”

Freedom properly means fearless, courageous people with clear minds and noble ideas. It does not mean that a handful from one particular community, few enough to count on one's fingers, sit upon the governing council.

### The Road to Freedom

By what method can this freedom be obtained? Intellectuals must explain to the people whose path can achieve it, lead them along that road, prevent them taking another and guide them towards the good path.

Yesterday we asked what intellectuals must do. Moonje calls us to the Vedic age; Jinnah shows a path according to Islamic character; Periyar calls us to the sacred Dravidian path. The people are bewildered. Which is right, and what is the intellectual's duty?

We must closely examine what kind of freedom a return to the Vedic age provides, and what freedom the other two positions provide. No one will disagree that the freedom we seek should resemble the poet's vision. Such freedom has substance; what use is an empty form?

We must therefore ask whether Moonje's road can attain it, or whether it lies on the paths shown by Jinnah and Periyar Ramasamy.

Jinnah and Periyar speak for people degraded in every sphere of society. Moonje speaks for those who for centuries have lived elevated and comfortable by keeping others backward. Other leaders argue for people suffering humiliation in this country; Moonje argues for the “earthly gods.” It is like crying that gruel needs salt while fretting that milk lacks sugar.

Who are you? An earthly god? If so, enter Moonje's camp without obstruction.

How can Muslims and Dravidians enter it? Can a deer caught in a net or an elephant fallen into a pit survive? Likewise, can Muslims and Dravidians live if caught by Moonje and others planning the foundation of Aryan rule? If you ask why Varadarajulu runs ahead of Moonje, we reply that even the handle of an axe is made of wood.

Let us now examine whether Moonje's argument is sound and whether it can yield the freedom described above.

### Moonje's Argument

Moonje spoke to school pupils about God, philosophy and religion. Aryanism alone owns the folly of inventing every imaginable tale about God's form and spreading delusion among people seeking grace. In an age of expanding knowledge and inquiry, the obscenities of Aryan stories have been exposed and people no longer believe them easily. Aryans have therefore been forced to bow before rationalists.

That submission explains why Moonje, speaking about God, mentioned neither the ten incarnations nor a figure clad in tiger skin and serpents. He said: “If God's nature is to be described, one must call it undiscoverable. Though countless people have made great efforts to see God since the world began, that remains His description.”

Yes. But how much foolishness have Aryan tales of God cultivated, and how completely have they obstructed the path of truth? Aryans invented without limit and destroyed reason. How many gods and goddesses, incarnations and divine exploits they describe! They left nothing untouched, claiming divinity in everything from human form to earth.

Siva's vehicle is a bull, so salute it. He wears a snake, while Hari sleeps upon Adisesha, so pour milk into anthills. Murugan has the peacock and Vinayaka the bandicoot. Kamadhenu grants every request. Garuda carries Vishnu, the cock marks Murugan's flag and the crow serves Saturn. Tulsi belongs to Vishnu, neem delights Mariamman, one kind of earth becomes sacred ash and another the Vaishnavite mark. Siva has three eyes, Murugan twelve and Brahma once had ten. Perhaps annoyed by so many, Siva plucked away one of Brahma's heads, leaving him eight eyes. This concerns the principal gods; the retinue would expand endlessly.

Sit under the wish-fulfilling tree and obtain whatever you think of. Kamadhenu grants anything requested, so goods never run short. Celestial women such as Rambha, Urvashi and Tilottama appear in dazzling ornaments that steal eye and mind. While Tumburu and Narada sing and Nandi plays the drum, they dance before thousand-eyed Indra. Pleasure never lacks. Nor need eyes tire, because one supposedly cannot close them there. Amid the dance, who thinks of food? There is neither hunger nor sleep.

These descriptions entice. To frighten, there are pits of fire, boiling iron, holes writhing with scorpions, oil presses, spiked wheels, red-hot statues and messengers of Yama with tusks and sword-like faces, ready to inflict dreadful punishments.

These are Aryan gods, their vehicles, the bliss granted by grace and the suffering that comes without it. Must you live believing them? Is this Dravidian culture?

God's nature is supposedly formless sublimity, yet Aryans have spun countless tales like these. Moonje's ideal is that the Aryans who produced such obscenity regain their ancient status. Do you want it?

Nor are irrational stories Aryanism's only beauty. In government, the king must obey Brahmins. Vedic priests chant, perform sacrifices and advise him; if crossed, they curse. Everyone else must follow hereditary caste duty without deviation.

Society has four varnas: Brahmin, Kshatriya, Vaishya and Shudra. God is pleased when the latter three submit to Brahmins, for Brahmins are earthly gods and Brahma created Shudras from his feet to serve them.

Economically, Shudras produce wealth, Vaishyas exchange goods and Kshatriyas prevent enemies carrying it away.

The Brahmin demands of the king: “We require wealth to honour Maheswara through a great sacrifice, build hermitages and fire altars, feast ascetics, give abundant gifts and gather sacrificial materials.” He does not ask in today's feeble voice, grinning with an almanac under his arm, because the “ruinous” Self-Respect Movement did not exist then. He lifts his face and extends his hand with authority enough to frighten the listener.

The king orders his minister to supply the sage. If the treasury holds funds, they are given; otherwise the people are commanded to provide them. They suffer in giving but may not complain, for complaint is sin and crime.

Do you desire this order? One group labours and another eats; one community wanders while another commits excess. Under Moonje's Aryan order there will always be divine fables, Brahmin brokerage in religion, kings subordinated to Aryanism, Brahmin superiority and others' degradation, and an economy in which we work while Brahmins eat.

Aryanism is dying, and Moonjes labour to revive it. Moonje declares it our duty to restore Vedic Sanatana Dharma to its original form and return it to the glory of “book and sword.” Reviving it produces precisely the condition described above. His book and sword are in truth the almanac and darbha grass. He asks that Brahmins rule through their glory.

He would not speak so had Brahmins been the majority and conquered a tiny minority. They form three in every hundred. Jinnah and Periyar speak for the other ninety-seven. Moonje wants ninety-seven enslaved beneath three Aryans. Periyar and Jinnah argue that no justice permits glory for three and ruin for the rest.

If it becomes clear that three are Aryan and ninety-seven Dravidian, Moonje's project dies before birth. He therefore separates eight crore Muslims from the ninety-seven and incites conflict between them and the rest, believing Aryans can survive behind the pretext “Hindu religion.” For what are Hindus summoned? To perform menial service for Aryans.

The central trick is this: those who cry “Hindus unite; Muslims are foreign and separate” are themselves outsiders who entered and corrupted this country. Muslims are Dravidian by race, Islamic by faith and people of this land.

Only by refusing this conspiracy to divide the army entering our path of struggle can we overthrow Aryanism and give Dravidians freedom, well-being and self-respect.

Ask those who preach Hindu revival and Hindu Mahasabha: “Who are you—Aryan, Dravidian or axe?”

We seek to free people caught in this long cruelty. This is our path of struggle.

The wealthy must provide the army means to travel it, for freedom is wealth superior to all they possess. To roll in riches while one's society is enslaved resembles a son playing upon a soft bed while his deranged mother falls into a sewer.

Educated people too must join the army instead of seeking only where personal benefit lies. Those with doubts should read Sir Shanmukham's university convocation address. We shall write about it on Monday.

(*Viduthalai*, 10 August 1940)

## Part 3

“Students, a great responsibility now rests upon you. The right to govern the world belongs to you. You have earned degrees; now spread education through the wide world and perform your duty.” Such speeches are customary at university convocations.

After listening, graduates make applying for jobs their occupation, are treated like withered fruit, and adopt a philosophy of despair through endless wandering.

In recent years convocation speakers have discussed this pitiable condition and offered whatever devices occurred to them. Some say mere academic education is useless and urge technical training. Others advise graduates not to languish in cities but serve villages, while still others speak of commerce, science and many fields.

Sir Shanmukham, performing this duty at the latest convocation, showed graduates a path of struggle. Rather than merely finding a means to survive, he urged them to improve the way of life and understand the difference between themselves and others.

### The Duty of Graduates

If earning a degree means seeking only a glittering life, neither such people nor their society can prosper. We do not mean graduates and others must never live comfortably. We mean they must demonstrate through their own lives, not sermons, that a cultivated life is better than a glittering one.

Fine clothes, cultivated manners, public praise, a motor car, a three-storey mansion, uniformed servants and a shining official beneath an electric fan may delight family and make relatives boast. But if a graduate enters service that reforms society, society, history and the intellectual world will praise and rejoice in that person. Graduates must examine this closely.

Just as so-called religious leaders seek worship and offerings from people rather than teaching them, graduates imagine education makes them a higher caste entitled to luxury. Unemployment has reduced this attitude somewhat—an example of evil occasionally producing good.

The educated must clarify the thinking of the uneducated. We desire this year's graduates to do so.

Graduates have read throughout history that social disorder ruins political conditions and that hiding it without remedy resembles covering fire with straw. Knowing disorder exists here, is it fitting that they refuse the work of ending it?

Sir Shanmukham told them: “The questions before you should be: What are the country's problems? Have I the maturity to understand them? If so, do I spend my strength, influence, intelligence and character for the country?”

Have graduates understood?

If they have, they must enter our path of struggle. Iron must be heated red and struck hard to assume the shape required. Nothing happens if one asks whether it should be struck or whether one should sit by the fire sweating.

Likewise, it is empty boasting to say that before removing social disorder we should not quarrel about society's complications, but must first rouse society and fight for self-rule. That yields no desired result; it resembles trying to awaken a person stupefied by opium.

False ideas spread among the educated have made those seeking social reform, self-respect and equality appear selfish, communal, servile and hungry for office. This does not harm the leaders concerned; they will not hide through fear of abuse. The country suffers.

As long as rights campaigners are suppressed by cries of “communalist” and “self-seeker,” national progress will remain obstructed. Sham nationalism cannot cure this. Educated people must understand and act.

They must study the demands of the League and Dravidian leaders and ask why they agitate. Non-Congress people complain that some stage the drama of patriotism to expand their dominance and make their class ride upon others. How is this hunting office?

### What Is the Reason?

When a Panagal or Bobbili became minister, nationalists beat their breasts and screamed “job hunter!” Why were they silent when an Iyer rolled in office?

Why did nationalists ward off the evil eye and mark the foreheads of Krishnamachariar becoming Diwan of Baroda, Gopalaswami Ayyangar of Kashmir and C. P. Ramaswami Iyer of Travancore, yet lamented when Shanmukham became Diwan of Cochin: “Is there no one in Cochin? Why should Coimbatore Shanmukham become its Diwan?”

Nationalists ask courageously how a white man may rule India and violate national honour. Why can they not ask how Brahmins claim superiority over everyone in every field?

Those who promised to shake militarily powerful British imperialism by “non-violent means” did not oppose Brahminism, which has no army and survives by exploiting ordinary people's ignorance. Did they support those who opposed it? Even if not, did they refrain from attacking them?

Why do heroes seeking self-rule hesitate to work for self-respect? Social unity is the foundation of self-rule; unity comes only through equality; equality comes only when Brahminism ends. The educated know this, yet nationalism deludes them. Sir Shanmukham made it clear.

### Medicine for Communal Conflict

Caste and class conflict have assumed a central place in our country. Almost nothing effective has been done to resolve the grievance; political reform has instead strengthened it. It therefore concerns political dominance more than religion.

This communal problem was not created by historians, revolutionaries or self-seekers. Appeals to patriotism and spiritual patience cannot solve it. The principal communities involved must seek the common good and make appropriate sacrifices. Those possessing political dominance must yield and share with others. That is the road to settlement. Sir Shanmukham's statement deserves close attention.

Judas—not Martin Luther—was an enemy and traitor to Jesus. Judas pretended discipleship and intimacy to secure selfish ends, then angrily betrayed Jesus when he failed. Martin Luther, by contrast, agonised that Christianity required changes suited to the age lest Jesus's noble principles decay, led a revolution and caused a new branch to flourish.

### Our Work Is Social Reform

Likewise, people who perform an autocratic varnashrama drama for selfish class interest under the name of patriotism are traitors to the country. We who labour to abolish Brahminism—which ruined the ancient land, keeps it weak, turns most people into walking corpses and confines them within superstition and blind doctrine—are not traitors.

Those repairing a road will have mud upon hands and feet; do not grow weary at the sight. A chariot of imagination cannot run upon the ground. Similarly, Congress cannot forget both country and devotion, merely shout “patriotism” and cause patriotism to arise.

Social reform is therefore our work. Our battle is to oppose and abolish varnashrama and free the majority from conspirators' net. That is the path shown by Periyar and Jinnah.

We lovingly ask comrades who heard or read Sir Shanmukham's evidence-filled address to enter our path, join our army and become commanders. Universities gave you B.A. and M.A. degrees. Come now and earn the title “commander in the struggle for social equality.”

A university degree may provide a job—or at least the job of seeking one. We invite you to share the greater work of removing society's defects. Here lies wealth greater than anything gained from sitting comfortably in office: the joy of knowing we participated in liberating people enslaved for thousands of years.

To stand beside the weak against the strong and become a warrior of victory is the degree graduates must now receive. It is ready here. Come; we shall confer it.

(*Viduthalai*, 12 August 1940)
